  "content": {
    "title": "Iowa rallies to end Nebraska's first NCAA tournament run, reach first Elite Eight since 1987",
    "text": "Story by Iowa rallies to end Nebraska's first NCAA tournament run, reach first Elite Eight since 1987 Iowa used a late run, and took advantage of a bad defensive blunder, to beat Nebraska 77-71 on Thursday night in the Sweet 16 Ryan Young Staff writer Fri, March 27, 2026 at 4:18 AM UTC · 5 min read For the first time in nearly four decades, Iowa is just a win away from the Final Four. The Hawkeyes rallied out of a double-digit hole to knock off Nebraska in a rare Big Ten Sweet 16 battle on Thursday night in Houston. Iowa used a late 15-6 run down the stretch, and took advantage of a brutal defensive blunder by the Cornhuskers, to pull out a 77-71 win. Iowa led for just more than two full minutes throughout the entire contest. Advertisement Advertisement Advertisement Now, the Hawkeyes will now head to the Elite Eight for the first time since 1987. They’ll t ake on No. 3 Illinois in the next round at the Toyota Center on Saturday night. Iowa rallies out of early hole to knock off Nebraska Nebraska was in full control for nearly the entire first half on Thursday night. The Huskers took the lead from the jump, opening on a 14-4 run that shut Iowa down almost completely in the opening minutes. Nebraska looked simply much more prepared for the game than Iowa did on both sides of the ball. But finally, it was Alvaro Folgueiras who sparked Iowa’s late run. Folgueiras, who hit the game-winner to lift Iowa past Florida in the second round, threw down a dunk through Nebraska big man Rienk Mast to get the game back within single digits just inside the five-minute mark. Bennett Stirtz, who went 0-of-9 from behind the arc against the Gators, then hit his first 3-pointer two possessions later. While Nebraska’s field goal drought finally ended, Iowa cut the Nebraska lead back to just a single possession at the break after Tate Sage drilled a 3-pointer at the halftime buzzer. Advertisement Advertisement Advertisement Sage had 11 points at the half, after making a trio of 3-pointers, and the Hawkeyes shot 60% from the field. Nebraska, with 11 from Pryce Sandfort, shot 58% from the field in the first 20 minutes. It marked the best combined shooting in a half so far throughout the entire NCAA tournament. That momentum Iowa picked up just before halftime carried over, too. The Hawkeyes quickly tied the game up by the first media timeout of the second period on a Sage layup, marking the first tie since the game had started. Iowa hung with Nebraska for more than 10 minutes that way, always catching up every time the Huskers pulled ahead slightly. But eventually, Stirtz gave the Hawkeyes their first lead of the game with just more than two minutes left. Stirtz drilled a heavily contested 3-pointer from the wing, putting the Hawkeyes up by three. Sage then followed suit after a stop, contributing to a 9-0 Iowa run that spanned more than five minutes. Suddenly, Iowa was up by six. That sparked the final run and eventually lifted Iowa to the six-point win. Advertisement Advertisement Advertisement Folgueiras and Iowa took full advantage of an awful Nebraska mistake down the stretch , too. Folgueiras converted a tough and-one layup on an inbound pass that put Iowa up by six with just less than 60 seconds left in the contest, but he did it with only four Huskers defenders on the floor. Nebraska was a man short after a timeout, which allowed Folgueiras to sneak past everyone. It’s unclear why Nebraska was a man short, but the damage was done. Sandfort led Nebraska with 25 points and five rebounds after he went 8-of-13 from the field. Braden Frager added 16 points. They were the only two Nebraska players to hit double figures. Nebraska, which started the season on a 20-game win streak, won its first ever NCAA tournament game last weekend, then added another win to reach the Sweet 16. Advertisement Advertisement Advertisement Stirtz led Iowa with 20 points, and Sage finished with 19 points and eight rebounds off the bench. The Hawkeyes went 13-of-30 from behind the arc and shot 52% from the field. How far can Iowa actually go? The Hawkeyes were in rough shape at the end of the season. They lost seven of their last 10 games, and were one of the final Big Ten teams to even make it into the tournament altogether. But now, the Hawkeyes are the lone “Cinderella” left standing in the tournament and have figured things out at just the right time. They are by far the lowest-seeded team left standing, thanks to Purdue’s game-winner to beat No. 11 Texas on Thursday night . The Hawkeyes are now the lowest-seeded Big Ten team to ever reach the Elite Eight round. Advertisement Advertisement Advertisement A Big Ten rematch against Illinois on Saturday undoubtedly help the Hawkeyes, too. Iowa came within just six points of beating Illinois in its only matchup in the regular season, and now gets to avoid the Cougars in their home city. It is, on paper, a much better matchup for them. But regardless of how it ends up, Iowa’s best NCAA tournament run in decades continues. Advertisement Advertisement",
